n with a chow or pair.\n- No Honors (1 point) \u2014 yes, I have no honors.\n- One Voided Suit (1 point) \u2014 I have W, T, B \u2014 all three suits, so no.\n- Self Drawn (1 point) \u2014 if I draw and win.\n- But total would be low \u2014 possibly \"Chicken Hand\" (8 points) if nothing else.\n\nBut Chicken Hand requires zero other patterns except basic win \u2014 but actually, Chicken Hand *is* the 8-point fallback pattern when you have no major patterns.\n\nPer rules: \"Chicken Hand: A hand which would otherwise be worth of zero fans...\" \u2014 so if I have minor patterns adding up, it doesn't count.\n\nSo better to aim for at least some structure.\n\nBut with current state, I'm likely heading for a low-scoring win.\n\nThus, safest discard is one that doesn\u2019t feed opponents.\n\nAmong valid actions, **Play W4** is acceptable, but let's see if any tile is particularly dangerous.\n\nJ3 is fully exposed (next player has Peng J3), and I discarded J3 earlier \u2014 safe.\n\nF3 is punged by previous player.\n\nB6 is tripled by across player \u2014 very dangerous.\n\nBut none of my discards are B6.\n\nNow, B1: only two left unshown (three were used by across\u2019s [B4,B5,B6]? Wait: [B4,B5,B6] uses one B6, across has [B6,B6,B6] \u2014 that\u2019s four B6 total? But only four exist.\n\nUnshown B6: 0 \u2192 yes, all accounted for.\n\nSimilarly, B5: unshown 1 \u2014 I have [B4,B5,B6] meld, so I used one B5. Others? Across had [B4,B5,B6] \u2014 wait, no: my meld is [B4,B5,B6], and across has [B6,B6,B6] \u2014 so B5: only one in play? But unshown says B5:1 \u2014 yes, one left.\n\nBut no one is claiming B5, so probably safe.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nThe best move is to preserve the **B1 pair** and discard a lonely W tile.\n\nBetween W2 and W4, both are equally safe, but W4 is slightly more central and thus slightly more useful to opponents (e.g., in Pure Straight). So W2 is safer.\n\nBut I already discarded W2 earlier (history: Play W2), and no one Chowed it \u2014 strong sign it's safe.\n\nSimilarly, I played W4 earlier \u2014 also safe.\n\nBut I must choose from valid actions.\n\nWait: valid actions include **Play B2**, **Play W4**, **Play B1**, **Play W2**.\n\nAll are technically playable.\n\nBut **Play B1** would break the pair \u2014 bad.\n\n**Play B2** removes a potential component.\n\n**Play W2** or **Play W4** \u2014 remove a useless W tile.\n\nSince I have both W2 and W4, and neither helps me, but I must keep one W tile? No \u2014 I can discard both eventually.\n\nBut I only need to discard one now.\n\nPrioritize keeping tiles that could form pairs.\n\nB1 pair > B2 singleton.\n\nSo keep B1 x2, keep B2, discard W tile.\n\nNow, which W tile?\n\nI have W2 and W4.\n\nTo complete a [W2,W3,W4] chow, I would need W3.\n\nW3 unshown: 1 \u2014 very rare.\n\nAnd I previously discarded W3 (history: Play W3), so likely safe.\n\nBut no one claimed it \u2014 supports safety.\n\nBut if I keep both W2 and W4, I could theoretically draw W3 and form a chow \u2014 but extremely unlikely.\n\nSo holding both is wasteful.\n\nBetter to discard one.\n\nWhich one is more likely to be called?\n\n[W2,W3,W4] is a standard chow \u2014 so discarding W2 could allow someone to Chi with W3+W4, or W4 with W2+W3.\n\nBut since W3 is rare, probability is low.\n\nSame for both.\n\nBut W4 is higher frequency in winning hands (e.g., 4-5-6 sequences).\n\nSo W2 is safer to discard.\n\nBut earlier I thought W4.\n\nLet me check frequency of use:\n\nIn common patterns:\n- Lower Tiles: 1,2,3 \u2014 W2 relevant\n- Middle: 4,5,6 \u2014 W4 relevant\n- Upper: 7,8,9 \u2014 neither\n\nBut I have no other W tiles.\n\nAlso, across player has W9, W5, W1 \u2014 suggests they might have been building something, but already formed two T melds and one B pung.\n\nTheir last action was Play T9 \u2014 so they are discarding.\n\nNext player has J3 kong and discarding W9, W3, etc.\n\nSo no strong W interest.\n\nConclusion: **discard W4**\n\nBecause:\n- It is less connected to the edges (1-2-3 or 7-8-9)\n- Slightly less commonly needed in pure forms\n- And I have no supporting tiles for it\n\nWhereas W2 could be part of a 1-2-3, which is more common.\n\nBut both are low risk.\n\nAlternatively, **Play B2** \u2014 but B2 is part of lower dots, and I have B1s, so keeping B2 allows [B1,B2,B3] possibility, however remote.\n\nSo better to keep B2 than W4.\n\nThus, discard **W4**.\n\nAnswer: Play W4\n\nWait \u2014 but valid action is \"Play W4\", yes.\n\nFinal decision.\n\nSituation: I have three chows already, hand consists of W2, W4, B1(x2), B2. Need a pair to win. Best pair prospect is B1. Opponents have exposed melds in other suits, with little sign of Character (W) interest. W4 is isolated and risky to keep as it offers no progression.\n\nEvaluation: \n- Play B1: breaks pair, poor choice. \n- Play B2: loses connection to B1/B3, but B3 is rare. \n- Play W2 or W4: both safe, but W4 is less useful in general strategy.\n\nReason: Discarding W4 minimizes risk to opponents while clearing deadwood. It has no synergy with my current hand, and the chance of forming [W3,W4,W5] is negligible due to lack of W3/W5. Preserving B1 pair and B2 offers slight improvement potential.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
